<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pietermaritzburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pietermaritzburg is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a> in 1838 and named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> leaders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piet-retief">Piet Retief</a> and Gerrit Maritz. It was the seat of the Natalia Republic and then of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colony-of-natal">Colony of Natal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/natal-province">Natal Province</a>. In 1893 Mohandas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> was put off a train at its station, an experience that shaped his campaigns against discrimination.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pietermaritzburg.jpg" alt="Pietermaritzburg" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Janek Szymanowski · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:00000-St_Peters_Church2-Pietermaritzburg-s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
